@startuml Topic9_Handoffs_and_Message_Schemas
' ============================================================
' TOPIC 9: Handoffs & Message Schemas (the boundary between agents)
' SILENT FAILURE is the most dangerous & most-tested idea.
' Verify BEFORE releasing control. 4 schema components.
' ============================================================
title Topic 9 — Handoffs & Message Schemas\n(The agent boundary — where multi-agent systems break)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12

start

:Sending agent must hand work to a receiving agent;
note right
  Handoff message = structured payload passed
  from sender to receiver.
  Why it matters: CONTEXT ISOLATION (Topic 6) —
  nothing crosses the boundary automatically,
  so everything needed must be EXPLICITLY included.
end note

partition "BUILD THE SCHEMA (4 components)" {
  :1. TASK DESCRIPTION — what to produce;
  :2. RELEVANT CONTEXT — only the facts needed;
  :3. OUTPUT FORMAT — exact structure of the result;
  :4. CONSTRAINTS — scope limits, safety/policy rules;
}

#Yellow:Apply INCLUDE vs EXCLUDE test;
note right
  Ask: "Does the receiver NEED this to
  complete the task?" If NO -> leave it out.
  EXCLUDE: full conversation history, sender's
  deliberation logs, internal state, redundant context.
  Target = MINIMAL BUT SUFFICIENT.
end note

if (Context level correct?) then (under-specified)
  #Pink:Receiver GUESSES -> hallucination /\nwrong output / silent failure;
elseif (over-specified) then
  #Pink:Bloat -> cost, latency,\nnoise competes with signal;
else (minimal but sufficient)
  #LightGreen:Good schema;
endif

partition "HANDOFF VERIFICATION (before releasing control)" {
  :Sender transmits the handoff message;
  :Receiver ACKNOWLEDGES receipt;
  :Receiver CONFIRMS it has the context it needs;
  :ONLY THEN sender releases control;
  note right
    Verification separates a COMPLETED handoff
    from an ASSUMED one — it makes success observable.
  end note
}

if (Did the handoff truly succeed?) then (transparent failure)
  #Orange:Error is RAISED — you KNOW it broke\n(recoverable);
else (SILENT FAILURE — most dangerous)
  #Pink:**SILENT FAILURE**;
  note right
    Looks successful, but loses/corrupts something
    critical, with NO error raised. Masquerades as
    success, so corruption propagates downstream and
    is very hard to trace. THE most-tested trap.
    Defenses: verification + AUDIT LOGGING
    (turns silent failures into diagnosable ones).
  end note
endif

#LightBlue:CHECKPOINT before every handoff;
note right
  Save task state to a durable checkpoint =
  last CONFIRMED GOOD state (not just latest attempt).
  Retry resumes from the checkpoint, not from scratch.
  Matters most in PIPELINE topologies (mid-line failure).
end note

stop

floating note left
  == 3 WAYS FREEFORM HANDOFFS FAIL ==
  1. Missing context (sender assumed receiver knew)
  2. Inconsistent format (receiver guesses at intent)
  3. Implicit assumptions (sender's model of receiver
     is wrong — surfaces only when it breaks) <- most dangerous
  ....
  == SCHEMA EVOLUTION ==
  * Version tag on every message (receiver knows how to parse)
  * Handle missing OPTIONAL fields gracefully
  * New REQUIRED field needs a migration path
  * Forward compatibility: new receiver can read old senders
  ....
  == TESTING & DESIGN ==
  * Contract tests: verify required fields present BEFORE dispatch
    (catch at dev time, not via live silent failures)
  * Design schema against an INTERFACE, not a specific agent
    -> receivers become SUBSTITUTABLE
end note

@enduml
